---
import ArticleLayout from '../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { PAGES, AGE_PAGE_AGES, agePage } from '../lib/pages.ts';
import { buildCanonicalUrl, buildCollectionPage, buildItemList } from '../lib/seo.ts';

const page = PAGES.choresByAge;
const itemListId = `${buildCanonicalUrl(page.path)}#itemlist`;
const items = AGE_PAGE_AGES.map((age) => {
  const p = agePage(age);
  return {
    name: p.label,
    url: buildCanonicalUrl(p.path),
    description: p.description,
  };
});
const collectionJsonLd = [
  buildCollectionPage({
    name: page.label,
    description: page.description,
    path: page.path,
    itemListId,
    datePublished: page.published,
    dateModified: page.lastmod,
  }),
  buildItemList(items, itemListId),
];
---

<ArticleLayout page={page} jsonLd={collectionJsonLd}>
  <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed">
    Finding the right chores by age is the secret to building responsible, confident kids without daily arguments. A well-designed chore chart by age matches tasks to your child's physical coordination, attention span, and emotional maturity. Whether you have an eager two-year-old wanting to imitate household routines or a pre-teen ready to manage their own laundry and prepare simple meals, introducing age-appropriate chores teaches practical life skills while helping children feel like valued contributors to the family home.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
    Explore our comprehensive breakdown of chores by age below, or select your child's exact age to open a dedicated guide with ready-made printable charts you can customize in your browser:
  </p>

  <section class="my-6 rounded-xl border border-line bg-card p-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-fg">Chores by specific age</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">
      Select an age for a dedicated guide, chore examples, and a printable chart preview ready to customize:
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-5">
      {
        AGE_PAGE_AGES.map((age) => (
          <a
            href={agePage(age).path}
            class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center rounded-lg border border-line bg-bg p-3 text-center transition hover:border-brand hover:shadow-xs"
          >
            <span class="text-lg font-bold text-fg">{age} Years</span>
            <span class="mt-1 text-xs text-muted">Chores & Chart</span>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-8 space-y-6">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-fg">Why matching chores to child age matters</h2>
    <p class="text-muted">
      When parents assign chores that are too complex, children become discouraged, frustrated, and resistant. Conversely, when tasks are too easy or parents do everything themselves, kids miss out on developing motor skills, executive function, and self-reliance. Calibrating chores by age ensures tasks are challenging enough to build confidence while remaining safely within your child's physical and developmental capabilities.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-fg">General chore milestones by age group</h2>

    <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5 space-y-2">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-fg">Toddlers (Ages 2–3): Imitation & sensory helpers</h3>
      <p class="text-muted">
        At this stage, chores are sensory learning activities. Toddlers love to mimic parents. Keep tasks short (2 to 3 minutes) with direct adult participation:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-sm text-muted">
        <li>Putting toys and plush animals into low open baskets.</li>
        <li>Dropping dirty clothes and pajamas into the laundry hamper.</li>
        <li>Wiping up water or milk spills using a small dry washcloth.</li>
        <li>Stacking sturdy cardboard board books on low shelves.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5 space-y-2">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-fg">Preschool & Kindergarten (Ages 4–5): Structured daily habits</h3>
      <p class="text-muted">
        Four and five-year-olds can follow two-step instructions and take pride in having designated responsibilities in their bedrooms and the kitchen:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-sm text-muted">
        <li>Pulling up bed sheets and arranging pillows each morning.</li>
        <li>Setting unbreakable plates, napkins, and utensils on the dinner table.</li>
        <li>Watering indoor potted plants using a small watering can.</li>
        <li>Matching clean socks by color and folding small washcloths.</li>
        <li>Putting away shoes and hanging backpacks on low entryway hooks.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5 space-y-2">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-fg">Early Elementary (Ages 6–7): Household team members</h3>
      <p class="text-muted">
        Children aged 6 and 7 have developed greater fine motor control and can complete tasks that require attention to detail across several rooms:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-sm text-muted">
        <li>Sweeping kitchen floors with a lightweight broom and dustpan.</li>
        <li>Feeding family pets on a consistent morning and evening schedule.</li>
        <li>Emptying bathroom and bedroom wastebaskets into the main trash.</li>
        <li>Putting away clean silverware and sorting utensils in the drawer.</li>
        <li>Packing snacks, water bottles, and homework folders into school bags.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5 space-y-2">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-fg">Upper Elementary (Ages 8–10): Independent helpers</h3>
      <p class="text-muted">
        Eight to ten-year-olds can execute multi-step household chores independently with minimal reminders when given a visual checklist:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-sm text-muted">
        <li>Loading and unloading the dishwasher safely.</li>
        <li>Vacuuming rugs and living areas.</li>
        <li>Wiping down kitchen counters and bathroom sinks with mild cleaners.</li>
        <li>Folding and putting away their own clean laundry into bedroom drawers.</li>
        <li>Taking wheelie trash and recycling bins to the curb on collection day.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5 space-y-2">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-fg">Middle School & Tweens (Ages 11–14): Full household contributions</h3>
      <p class="text-muted">
        Older kids and young teens are capable of managing complete household systems that prepare them for future independent adulthood:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-sm text-muted">
        <li>Doing their own laundry from sorting and washing to folding and putting away.</li>
        <li>Deep-cleaning bathrooms, including scrubbing toilets, showers, and mirrors.</li>
        <li>Preparing simple family meals from scratch (such as pasta, scrambled eggs, or wraps).</li>
        <li>Mowing the lawn and yard maintenance with proper adult safety supervision.</li>
        <li>Changing bed linens and organizing closets and pantries.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-fg">How to teach chores without power struggles</h2>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-muted">
      <li>
        <strong>Teach with the "I Do, We Do, You Do" model:</strong> First demonstrate the chore completely while explaining your steps. Next, perform the chore together as a team. Finally, observe your child completing it independently and offer praise.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Accept kid-sized standards:</strong> A bed made by a six-year-old will have wrinkles, and towels folded by an eight-year-old will not be boutique-perfect. Avoid re-doing their work immediately in front of them, which communicates that their effort was worthless.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Anchor chores to daily rhythms:</strong> Tie tasks to established daily events rather than vague times—such as "make bed immediately after getting dressed" or "clear plate directly after dinner".
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Use visual picture charts:</strong> Visual checklists remove the parent from the nagging loop. The chore chart by age becomes the objective guide that children can check off with satisfaction.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10 border-t border-line pt-8 space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-fg">Create a custom printable chart</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-muted">Turn any age-appropriate routine into a free printable chart for your home:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-base">
      <li>
        <a href={PAGES.choreChartMaker.path} class="text-brand underline font-medium">Free Chore Chart Maker</a>:
        build a weekly picture chart with customized icons, points, or allowance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={PAGES.rewardChartMaker.path} class="text-brand underline font-medium">Reward Chart Maker</a>:
        motivate new habits with sticker tracks and fun milestone rewards.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={PAGES.morningRoutineChart.path} class="text-brand underline font-medium">Morning Routine Chart</a>:
        smooth out hectic school mornings with picture steps in sequential order.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={PAGES.bedtimeRoutineChart.path} class="text-brand underline font-medium">Bedtime Routine Chart</a>:
        peaceful evening checklists that guide children through bedtime wind-down.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={PAGES.familyChoreChart.path} class="text-brand underline font-medium">Family Chore Chart</a>:
        organize household responsibilities across parents and multiple siblings.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={PAGES.freeChoreChartTemplate.path} class="text-brand underline font-medium">Free Chore Chart Templates</a>:
        ready-to-print starting points for busy families.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</ArticleLayout>
